  "response": "The most likely cause is `direct_date` in `src/billing.ts`: seven wall-clock reads, including several inside the 204-line `generateInvoice`. If the operation crosses midnight between `Date.now()` calls, different parts of one invoice can use different dates, making boundary tests flaky.\n\nProposed fix:\n\n- Inject a `Clock`/`now()` dependency.\n- Capture `now` once at the start of `generateInvoice`.\n- Derive every timestamp and date comparison from that snapshot.\n- Use one time domain—prefer UTC for billing calculations.\n- In tests, pass a fixed instant immediately before/after midnight and assert exact invoice dates.\n\n```ts\ninterface Clock {\n  now(): Date;\n}\n\nconst systemClock: Clock = {\n  now: () => new Date(),\n};\n\nfunction generateInvoice(input: InvoiceInput, clock: Clock = systemClock) {\n  const now = clock.now();\n  const timestamp = now.getTime();\n\n  // Use `now` or `timestamp` throughout—no further Date.now()/new Date().\n}\n```\n\nAlso investigate any billing imports from `src/date.ts`: that module mixes UTC and local methods and parses timezone-less strings, both credible boundary failures. Standardize those helpers on UTC and reject or explicitly zone ambiguous date strings.\n\nThe large `generateInvoice` function increases the chance that scattered clock reads interact unnoticed, while the billing tests provide weak protection. Add focused cases for UTC midnight, the relevant business timezone’s midnight, month/year rollover, and—if local calendar dates matter—DST transitions.",
